{"title":"KVPY","category":"Engineering","bank":"kvpy","subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","count":26,"questions":[{"id":50981,"subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","exam":"Engineering","examName":"KVPY","q":"The most polarisable ion among the following is.","options":["$F^{-}$","$I^{-}$","${Na}^{+}$","${Cl}^{-}$"],"answer":1,"solution":"& Among anions with same charge, the one having greatest size has maximum polarisability. Thus, $I^{-}$& ion having most polarisability.","difficulty":"Medium","source":"KVPY 2019 (SA)","_marksId":"614da486ef8ddba76a47a7ca"},{"id":50982,"subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","exam":"Engineering","examName":"KVPY","q":"Among the following molecules, the one with the largest bond angle at the central atom is","options":["$\\mathrm{ClF}_{3}$","$\\mathrm{POCl}_{3}$","$\\mathrm{BCl}_{3}$","$\\mathrm{SO}_{3}$"],"answer":0,"solution":"<img src=\"/api/proxy-image?url=https%3A%2F%2Ffirebasestorage.googleapis.com%2Fv0%2Fb%2Fquantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app%2Fo%2Fquestions%252Ffigs%252Fpyq%252Fkvpy%252FZKx0Kq0tnY2l9YfeP7AnSjxrETyCUXscWtyCrnkcCBo.original.fullsize.png%3Falt%3Dmedia&clean=1&v=qxfig138\" data-qx-orig-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fkvpy%2FZKx0Kq0tnY2l9YfeP7AnSjxrETyCUXscWtyCrnkcCBo.original.fullsize.png?alt=media\" data-qx-storage-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fkvpy%2FZKx0Kq0tnY2l9YfeP7AnSjxrETyCUXscWtyCrnkcCBo.original.fullsize.png?alt=media\">","difficulty":"Medium","source":"KVPY 2017 (19 Nov SA)","_marksId":"6174d213514c47650aa0e8e2","question":"Among the following molecules, the one with the largest bond angle at the central atom is","explanation":"<img src=\"/api/proxy-image?url=https%3A%2F%2Ffirebasestorage.googleapis.com%2Fv0%2Fb%2Fquantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app%2Fo%2Fquestions%252Ffigs%252Fpyq%252Fkvpy%252FZKx0Kq0tnY2l9YfeP7AnSjxrETyCUXscWtyCrnkcCBo.original.fullsize.png%3Falt%3Dmedia&clean=1&v=qxfig138\" data-qx-orig-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fkvpy%2FZKx0Kq0tnY2l9YfeP7AnSjxrETyCUXscWtyCrnkcCBo.original.fullsize.png?alt=media\" data-qx-storage-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fkvpy%2FZKx0Kq0tnY2l9YfeP7AnSjxrETyCUXscWtyCrnkcCBo.original.fullsize.png?alt=media\">"},{"id":50983,"subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","exam":"Engineering","examName":"KVPY","q":"Among the following, the most polar molecule is :","options":["${AlCl}_{3}$","${CCl}_{4}$","${SeCl}_{6}$","${AsCl}_{3}$"],"answer":3,"solution":"<p>$\\begin{cases}{AlCl}_{3} & non - polar\\end{cases}$(trigonal planar molecule with no lone pair of electrons)</p> <p>$\\begin{cases}{CCl}_{4} & non - polar\\end{cases}$(Tetrahedral molecule with no lone pair of electrons)</p> <p>$\\begin{cases}{SeCl}_{6} & non - polar\\end{cases}$(Octahedral molecule with no lone pair of electrons)</p> <p>$\\begin{cases}{AsCl}_{3} & polar\\end{cases}$(pyramidal molecule with one lone pair of electrons)</p>","difficulty":"Medium","source":"KVPY 2020 (SA)","_marksId":"614da4f5e3c8fe1cff238b82"},{"id":50984,"subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","exam":"Engineering","examName":"KVPY","q":"The covalent characters of ${CaCl}_{2}{BaCl}_{2}, {SrCl}_{2}$ and ${MgCl}_{2}$ follow the order :","options":["${CaCl}_{2} < {BaCl}_{2} < {SrCl}_{2} < {MgCl}_{2}$","${BaCl}_{2} < {SrCl}_{2}, < {CaCl}_{2} < {MgCl}_{2}$","${CaCl}_{2} < {BaCl}_{2} < {MgCl}_{2} < {SrCl}_{2}$","${SrCl}_{2} < {MgCl}_{2} < {CaCl}_{2} < {BaCl}_{2}$"],"answer":1,"solution":"<p>$\\begin{cases}{MgCl}_{2} & {CaCl}_{2} & {SrCl}_{2} & {BaCl}_{2}\\end{cases}$<br />\nMore the polarising power of the cation, more is the covalent character. Smaller the cation, more is the polarising power.<br />\n${BaCl}_{2} < {SrCl}_{2} < {CaCl}_{2} < {MgCl}_{2}$ (Covalent character)</p>","difficulty":"Medium","source":"KVPY 2020 (SA)","_marksId":"614da4f5be07a250b91fb492"},{"id":50985,"subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","exam":"Engineering","examName":"KVPY","q":"<p>The hybridisations of $N, C$ and $O$ shown in the following compound</p>\n<p><img src=\"/api/proxy-image?url=https%3A%2F%2Ffirebasestorage.googleapis.com%2Fv0%2Fb%2Fquantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app%2Fo%2Fquestions%252Ffigs%252Fpyq%252Fkvpy%252F34697002-7069-4421-9981-ca527c04bf40-image-20201225135343-1.png%3Falt%3Dmedia&clean=1&v=wm2\" data-qx-orig-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fkvpy%2F34697002-7069-4421-9981-ca527c04bf40-image-20201225135343-1.png?alt=media\" data-qx-storage-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fkvpy%2F34697002-7069-4421-9981-ca527c04bf40-image-20201225135343-1.png?alt=media\"/></p>\n<p>respectively, are<br/> </p>","options":["$sp^{2}, sp, sp^{2}$& "," $sp^{2}, sp^{2}, sp^{2}$<br />\n& ","$sp^{2}, sp, sp$","$sp, sp, sp^{2}$"],"answer":0,"solution":"<p>Hybridisation is determined from the steric number (number of atoms bonded to the central atom + the number of lone pairs). Number of hybrid orbitals must be equal to the steric number.<br/>\nFrom the Lewis structure.</p>\n<p><img src=\"/api/proxy-image?url=https%3A%2F%2Ffirebasestorage.googleapis.com%2Fv0%2Fb%2Fquantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app%2Fo%2Fquestions%252Ffigs%252Fpyq%252Fkvpy%252F3e132b99-404c-47ad-bd4c-2566e164039f-image-20201225135529-2.png%3Falt%3Dmedia&clean=1&v=wm2\" data-qx-orig-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fkvpy%2F3e132b99-404c-47ad-bd4c-2566e164039f-image-20201225135529-2.png?alt=media\" data-qx-storage-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fkvpy%2F3e132b99-404c-47ad-bd4c-2566e164039f-image-20201225135529-2.png?alt=media\"/></p>\n<p>(i) Steric number of N-atom $= 3$ (2 bonded atoms +1 lone pair), $∴$ Hybridisation $= sp^{2}$ (3 hybrid orbitals).<br/>\n(ii) Steric number of C-atom $= 2$ (2 bonded atoms),<br/>\n$∴$: Hybridisation $= sp$ ( 2 hybrid orbitals).<br/>\n(iii) Steric number of 0 -atom $= 3$ (1 bonded atom +2 lone pair) $∴$ Hybridisation $= {sp}^{2} ( 3$ hybrid orbitals).<br/> </p>","difficulty":"Medium","source":"KVPY 2019 (SA)","_marksId":"614da483333325a5fd46a8e1"},{"id":50986,"subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","exam":"Engineering","examName":"KVPY","q":"Among the following species, the $H - X - H$ angle ( $X = B, N$ or $P$& ) follows the order.","options":["${PH}_{3} < {NH}_{3} < {NH}_{4}^{+} < {BF}_{3}$","${NH}_{3} < {PH}_{3} < {NH}_{4}^{+} < {BF}_{3}$","${BF}_{3} < {PH}_{3} < {NH}_{4}^{+} < {NH}_{3}$","${BF}_{3} < {NH}_{4}^{+} < {NH}_{3} < {PH}_{3}$"],"answer":0,"solution":"<p>As the electronegativity of the central atom increases the bond angle also increases. So, ${NH}_{3}$ will have high bond angle than ${PH}_{3}$. The bond angle of ${NH}_{4}^{+}$ is highest because of its symmetrical geometry, also bond pair and lone pair on $N$ of ${NH}_{3}$ suffer repulsion, which decreases its bond angle in comparison to ${NH}_{4}^{+}$. Thus, the correct order of bond angle is</p> <p>${PH}_{3} < {NH}_{3} < {NH}_{4}^{+}$</p> <p>But in the given question none of the option is correct because there is no $H - X - H$ bond angle in ${BF}_{3}$</p>","difficulty":"Medium","source":"KVPY 2018 (SB/SX)","_marksId":"614da68bcb6d117225d5cba9"},{"id":50987,"subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","exam":"Engineering","examName":"KVPY","q":"The $F - Br - F$ bond angles in $Br{F}_{5}$ and the $Cl - P - Cl$ bond angles in $PC{l}_{5}$, respectively, are","options":["identical in $Br{F}_{5}$ but non-identical in $PC{l}_{5}$","identical in $Br{F}_{5}$ and identical in $PC{l}_{5}$<br />\n& ","non-identical in $Br{F}_{5}$ but identical in $PC{l}_{5}$","non-identical in $Br{F}_{5}$ and non-identical in $PC{l}_{5}$"],"answer":3,"solution":"<p>The geometry of $Br{F}_{5}$ is square pyramidal.</p>\n<p><img src=\"/api/proxy-image?url=https%3A%2F%2Ffirebasestorage.googleapis.com%2Fv0%2Fb%2Fquantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app%2Fo%2Fquestions%252Ffigs%252Fpyq%252Fkvpy%252Fbf7a7724-7687-4cda-8f4f-e07d7348fd0b-image.png%3Falt%3Dmedia&clean=1&v=wm2\" data-qx-orig-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fkvpy%2Fbf7a7724-7687-4cda-8f4f-e07d7348fd0b-image.png?alt=media\" data-qx-storage-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fkvpy%2Fbf7a7724-7687-4cda-8f4f-e07d7348fd0b-image.png?alt=media\"/></p>\n<p>Here, the lone pair occupies the axial position and hence axial bonds will suffer more repulsion than equatorial bonds. Thus, the axial $Br - F$ bond length will be different from equatorial $Br - F$.</p>\n<p>The geometry of $PC{l}_{5}$ is triangular pyramidal.</p>\n<p><img src=\"/api/proxy-image?url=https%3A%2F%2Ffirebasestorage.googleapis.com%2Fv0%2Fb%2Fquantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app%2Fo%2Fquestions%252Ffigs%252Fpyq%252Fkvpy%252Fd7d7ca6d-429e-4e5d-8930-f1efa51e4731-image.png%3Falt%3Dmedia&clean=1&v=wm2\" data-qx-orig-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fkvpy%2Fd7d7ca6d-429e-4e5d-8930-f1efa51e4731-image.png?alt=media\" data-qx-storage-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fkvpy%2Fd7d7ca6d-429e-4e5d-8930-f1efa51e4731-image.png?alt=media\"/></p>\n<p>The axial bonds suffer more repulsions than equatorial bonds, so they are larger in bond length.</p>","difficulty":"Medium","source":"KVPY 2018 (SA)","_marksId":"614da3ff655f0986ef2556e9"},{"id":50988,"subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","exam":"Engineering","examName":"KVPY","q":"If an extra electron is added to the hypothetical molecule ${C}_{2},$& this extra electron will occupy the molecular orbital:-","options":["${\\pi2P}^{*}$","${\\pi}_{2P}$","${\\sigma2P}^{*}$","${&#963;}_{2P}$"],"answer":3,"solution":"Configuration of ${C}_{2}$<br />\n${&#963;}_{1s}^{2}{&#963;}_{1s}^{* 2}{&#963;}_{2s}^{2}{&#963;}_{2s}^{* 2}{\\pi}_{2px}^{2} = {\\pi}_{2py}^{2}{&#963;}_{2pz}$<br />\nan extra electron added to the ${&#963;}_{2p}$ of the above configuration.","difficulty":"Medium","source":"KVPY 2020 (SB/SX)","_marksId":"614da7489d411806185ae5e6"},{"id":50989,"subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","exam":"Engineering","examName":"KVPY","q":"The diamagnetic species among the following is& ","options":["${O}_{2}^{+}$","${O}_{2}^{-}$","${O}_{2}$","${O}_{2}^{2 -}$"],"answer":3,"solution":"<p>To be diamagnetic, no unpaired electron must be present.& </p> <p>Hint Write molecular orbital electronic configuration of each species.</p> <p>For all options given,</p> <p>${&#963;}_{1s}^{2}{&#963;}_{1s}^{⋆ 2}{&#963;}_{2s}^{2}{&#963;}_{2s}^{* 2}{&#963;}_{2{p}_{z}}^{2}{\\pi}_{2{p}_{x}}^{2} = {\\pi}_{2{p}_{y}}^{2}$ is common.& </p> <p>After that, ${O}_{2}^{+}: {\\pi}_{2{p}_{x}}^{* 1}$,</p> <p>${O}_{2}^{-}: {\\pi}_{2{p}_{x}}^{* 2} = {\\pi}_{2{p}_{y}}^{* 1}$,</p> <p>and ${O}_{2}: {\\pi}_{2{p}_{x}}^{* 1} = {\\pi}_{2{p}_{y}}^{* 1}$,</p> <p>all contain unpaired electron. They are paramagnetic, but ${O}_{2}^{2 -}: {\\pi}_{2{p}_{x}}^{* 2} = {\\pi}_{2{p}_{y}}^{⋆ 2}$ has no unpaired electrons. </p> <p>$∴$ It is diamagnetic.</p>","difficulty":"Medium","source":"KVPY 2019 (SB/SX)","_marksId":"614da6f3f34a49dd6b7f9865"},{"id":50990,"subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","exam":"Engineering","examName":"KVPY","q":"Among the following atomic orhital overlap, the non-honding overlap is","options":["<img src=\"/api/proxy-image?url=https%3A%2F%2Ffirebasestorage.googleapis.com%2Fv0%2Fb%2Fquantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app%2Fo%2Fquestions%252Ffigs%252Fpyq%252Fkvpy%252Fpu0RN0-9dP49hoS3bgtQBdxdKfve_bvrM8mZnV2ePaw.original.fullsize.png%3Falt%3Dmedia&clean=1&v=wm2\" data-qx-orig-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fkvpy%2Fpu0RN0-9dP49hoS3bgtQBdxdKfve_bvrM8mZnV2ePaw.original.fullsize.png?alt=media\" data-qx-storage-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fkvpy%2Fpu0RN0-9dP49hoS3bgtQBdxdKfve_bvrM8mZnV2ePaw.original.fullsize.png?alt=media\">","<img src=\"/api/proxy-image?url=https%3A%2F%2Ffirebasestorage.googleapis.com%2Fv0%2Fb%2Fquantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app%2Fo%2Fquestions%252Ffigs%252Fpyq%252Fkvpy%252FYEggnazLrpOzj7GcgBzvnUeUAOHY1aMbyKIv57G62UE.original.fullsize.png%3Falt%3Dmedia&clean=1&v=wm2\" data-qx-orig-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fkvpy%2FYEggnazLrpOzj7GcgBzvnUeUAOHY1aMbyKIv57G62UE.original.fullsize.png?alt=media\" data-qx-storage-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fkvpy%2FYEggnazLrpOzj7GcgBzvnUeUAOHY1aMbyKIv57G62UE.original.fullsize.png?alt=media\">","<img src=\"/api/proxy-image?url=https%3A%2F%2Ffirebasestorage.googleapis.com%2Fv0%2Fb%2Fquantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app%2Fo%2Fquestions%252Ffigs%252Fpyq%252Fkvpy%252FAsWNPuHF5rBofd9UcjWg7pfBqHDk6oNeipR-eqApGRc.original.fullsize.png%3Falt%3Dmedia&clean=1&v=wm2\" data-qx-orig-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b